WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.621
The problem embeddings solve.

2
00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:09.773
Studio 1 ended with a limit you can’t prompt your way around: a model only knows what is in its weights or its context window.

3
00:00:10.053 --> 00:00:17.691
To answer questions about your company’s documents, you have to find the right few passages among thousands and put them in the prompt.

4
00:00:17.971 --> 00:00:21.043
That is retrieval, and this studio is about doing it well.

5
00:00:21.323 --> 00:00:25.056
Keyword search finds passages that share words with the question.

6
00:00:25.336 --> 00:00:28.408
It works surprisingly well, and you will use it in week 3.

7
00:00:28.688 --> 00:00:35.664
But it misses a question like “my package never showed up” when the help article is titled “Late or missing deliveries.

8
00:00:35.944 --> 00:00:38.312
” The words differ; the meaning is the same.

9
00:00:38.592 --> 00:00:40.896
An embedding model addresses that gap.

10
00:00:41.176 --> 00:00:52.269
It is a function that maps a piece of text to a fixed-length vector of real numbers, say 1,024 of them, trained so that texts with similar meaning land near each other.

11
00:00:52.549 --> 00:01:00.891
Once every document is a point in that space, “find documents about this question” becomes “find the points nearest to the question’s point.

12
00:01:01.171 --> 00:01:05.992
” That is a geometry problem, and you have the background to reason about it precisely.

13
00:01:06.272 --> 00:01:08.149
Two properties make this practical.

14
00:01:08.429 --> 00:01:18.051
Every input, a three-word query or a two-page policy, becomes a vector of the same length, so comparing any two texts costs one vector operation.

15
00:01:18.331 --> 00:01:22.704
And the document vectors are computed once, at indexing time, and stored.

16
00:01:22.984 --> 00:01:25.437
At query time you embed only the question.

17
00:01:25.717 --> 00:01:35.851
This week covers the vectors and how to compare them (this lesson), how to choose a model with evidence (lesson 2), and how to search millions of vectors quickly (lesson 3).
